\chapter{The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} Compile-Time Regex Engine}

Chapter 8 introduced the \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n} /\allowbreak{} \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} dispatch machinery and showed that a \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} clause can carry a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} key in addition to the more conventional value-based keys. That chapter treated the pattern key as an opaque token: something with a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} member that the dispatch table invokes behind the scenes. This chapter opens the box. It documents the \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>} template in full, explains the matching algorithm it implements, and shows the precise regex subset it supports.

\texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>} is a compile-time, regex-like matcher intended for short classification tasks: distinguishing an integer literal from an identifier, recognising a keyword, or routing a token kind. It is not a general regular-expression engine. It deliberately implements only a small, well-defined subset of regex syntax, just enough to express the character classes and quantifiers that show up in lexical micro-grammars. For richer recognition the library provides the PEG engine described in Chapters 27 and 28, which supports sequencing, alternation, and recursive grammars. This chapter is concerned solely with the lightweight \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} facility.

The design goal is to keep the matcher small enough to live entirely in a header, to be \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}-evaluable, and to require no allocation. Every match runs against \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} inputs and produces a \texttt{b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l}. There is no capture, no grouping, and no state carried between calls. The matcher is a pure function of two strings: the pattern, baked into the type at compile time, and the input, supplied at the call site.

\section{The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>} Template}

\texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n} is a class template parameterised by a non-type template parameter of type \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}. The \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} NTTP (Chapter 4) is what makes it possible to write \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} directly at the source level: the string literal is converted into a compile-time constant that can be carried as a template argument. The pattern text is therefore fixed for the lifetime of the type. Two distinct pattern strings denote two distinct types, and the compiler is free to instantiate each exactly once.

The public surface of the template is intentionally tiny. It exposes two \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} members: a \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} of type \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} that mirrors the pattern text, and a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} function that tests whether a given input satisfies the pattern. Everything else is private. The following block shows the template definition in the form a user would reproduce in a manual page.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
namespace dsl {

template <FixedString S> struct pattern
{
  static constexpr std::string_view value = S.view ();

  static constexpr bool matches (std::string_view input)
  {
    return match_here (S.view (), input);
  }

  // ... private helpers ...
};

} // namespace dsl
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} member is provided as a convenience for diagnostics, logging, and table construction. It is the textual form of the pattern, recovered from the \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} NTTP via \texttt{S\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. Because it is \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, taking its address or binding it to a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} parameter costs nothing at run time. The \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} member is the operational interface. It delegates to the private recursive matcher \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, passing both the pattern text and the input. The recursion is the heart of the engine and is examined in detail below.

Because both members are \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, the matcher can be used in \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} branches, in \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} functions, and in any other context that demands a compile-time result. The example shipped as \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} uses it from a runtime lambda, but the same calls are equally valid inside a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} context. The matcher performs no heap allocation, no threading, and no I/\allowbreak{}O; it is a pure function over two \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} ranges.

\section{Anchored Full-Match Semantics}

The most important semantic fact about \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} is that the match is anchored at both ends. A pattern matches an input if and only if the entire input, from the first character to the last, is described by the pattern. There is no notion of a partial or substring match. This is the same convention used by \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} rather than \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}. Users coming from ECMAScript regex semantics, where matches default to searching, should note the difference.

The anchoring has two practical consequences. First, a pattern such as \texttt{"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"} does not match the input \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}3\allowbreak{}"}; it matches \texttt{"\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}3\allowbreak{}"} but not a string that merely contains \texttt{"\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}3\allowbreak{}"}. Second, the engine does not need a \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} or \texttt{\$} anchor to express full matching, because full matching is the default. The characters \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} are accepted by the parser but are silently ignored: they are convenience tokens for users who like to write anchors explicitly, not operational directives.

This anchoring decision is what makes \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} usable as a classification predicate in a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table. A \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}>} clause asserts that the \emph{entire} key matches the integer pattern, not merely that the key contains an integer somewhere. That gives dispatch a precise, total-predicate semantics: for any given input key, at most one pattern in a well-formed table will match, provided the patterns are mutually exclusive. The engine itself does not enforce mutual exclusivity, but the anchoring makes it straightforward to achieve by construction.

The decision also keeps the matcher small: it starts at offset zero rather than searching every input position. Simple patterns are inexpensive, but multiple overlapping repetitions can require combinatorial backtracking. Use \texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x} for larger regular-expression workloads.

\section{Supported Syntax Subset}

The engine recognises a deliberately small grammar. The supported tokens are: the wildcard \texttt{.}, which matches any single character; a character class \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}]}, which matches any one of the listed characters; a range \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]}, which appears inside a class and matches any character in the inclusive range; the postfix quantifier \texttt{*}, meaning zero or more of the preceding atom; and the postfix quantifier \texttt{+}, meaning one or more of the preceding atom. The anchors \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} are parsed and discarded.

Each of these tokens is documented in the comment block attached to the \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} member, which is reproduced here for reference.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  /**
   * @brief Minimal regex-like match: anchored prefix/suffix, literal chars,
   * [class], *, +.
   *
   * Supported syntax (subset):
   *   .       any character
   *   [abc]   character class
   *   [a-z]   range in class
   *   *       zero or more of previous
   *   +       one or more of previous
   *   ^       (ignored, match is always anchored at start)
   *   $       (ignored, match is always anchored at end)
   *
   * @param input String to test.
   * @return true if the entire input matches the pattern.
   */
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Any character that is not one of these special tokens is treated as a literal. The literal \texttt{'\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}'} matches the character \texttt{'\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}'} and nothing else. Literals are case-sensitive; the engine has no case-insensitive mode. Because there is no escape mechanism, the characters \texttt{.} \texttt{[} \texttt{]} \texttt{*} \texttt{+} \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} \texttt{\$} \texttt{-} cannot be matched as literals when they appear in a position where the parser would interpret them as operators. This is one of the trade-offs of keeping the grammar small; users who need to match these characters literally should use the PEG engine (Chapter 27) instead.

The quantifiers \texttt{*} and \texttt{+} apply to the immediately preceding atom. An atom is either a single literal character, the \texttt{.} wildcard, or a complete \texttt{[\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]} class. Quantifiers do not compose: \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}+} is not meaningful and is not supported. There is no \texttt{?} quantifier, no \texttt{\{\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\}} counted repetition, and no grouping construct that would allow a quantifier to apply to a sub-expression of more than one atom.

\section{The \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s} Helper}

Character classes are handled by a dedicated helper, \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s}. The helper takes two arguments: the body of the class (the text between the brackets, with the brackets themselves stripped), and a single candidate character. It returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} if the candidate matches any element of the class, and \texttt{f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} otherwise. The body is scanned left to right, and the function returns on the first match.

The scan alternates between two interpretations of the body. At each position it checks whether the next three characters form a range, that is, whether the character two positions ahead is a hyphen. If so, the candidate is tested against the inclusive range from the current character to the character after the hyphen, and the scan advances by three. Otherwise the current character is treated as a singleton and the scan advances by one. The following definition is the verbatim form found in the header.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  static constexpr bool
  match_class (std::string_view cls, char c)
  {
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < cls.size ();)
      {
        if (i + 2 < cls.size () && cls[i + 1] == '-')
          {
            if (c >= cls[i] && c <= cls[i + 2])
              return true;
            i += 3;
          }
        else
          {
            if (cls[i] == c)
              return true;
            ++i;
          }
      }
    return false;
  }
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

A range is recognised by the presence of a hyphen at offset \texttt{i\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}1}, with a character available at offset \texttt{i\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}2}. The check \texttt{i\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} guards against a trailing hyphen at the end of the class body; in that case the hyphen is treated as a literal singleton because the range form is not available. This is why a pattern such as \texttt{[\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}]} matches either a hyphen or the letter \texttt{a}, and why \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}]} matches either an \texttt{a} or a hyphen. The behaviour falls out of the index test, not from a special case.

Classes may mix singletons and ranges freely. The pattern \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}]} matches any ASCII letter, upper or lower case, or an underscore. The pattern \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]} matches a digit or a full stop; here the \texttt{.} inside a class is a literal, because \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s} does not interpret it as a wildcard. Inside a class, every non-hyphen character is literal. This is a useful rule to remember: the wildcard meaning of \texttt{.} applies only outside classes.

The class engine has no notion of negation. There is no \texttt{[\allowbreak{}\textasciicircum{}\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}]} form. A class always asserts membership, never exclusion. Users who need to recognise "anything except a digit" must phrase the pattern differently, typically by listing the permitted characters explicitly or by structuring the surrounding \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table so that an earlier clause consumes the digit case and a later clause handles the remainder.

\section{The \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e} Helper}

Once the matcher has decided which atom is next in the pattern, it needs a uniform way to test whether a given input character satisfies that atom. That is the role of \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}. The helper takes three arguments: a \texttt{c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r} indicating the kind of atom (\texttt{'\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}'} for a class, \texttt{'\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}'} for the wildcard, or any other character for a literal), the class body \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s} (used only when the first argument is \texttt{'\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}'}), and the candidate character \texttt{c}. It returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} if the candidate satisfies the atom.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  static constexpr bool
  match_one (char pat, std::string_view cls, char c)
  {
    if (pat == '[')
      return match_class (cls, c);
    if (pat == '.')
      return true;
    return pat == c;
  }
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The three cases are mutually exclusive and are tested in order. A class atom delegates to \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s} with the precomputed body. The wildcard atom unconditionally returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}, because \texttt{.} matches any character, including newline or NUL should either appear in the input. A literal atom performs a single character comparison. The helper is small enough to be inlined trivially, and the compiler typically folds the dispatch into the surrounding recursion.

The reason \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e} takes both the atom kind and the class body as separate arguments, rather than taking the pattern substring directly, is that the surrounding \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e} has already parsed the class once and stored the body in a local \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s} variable. Passing the body forward avoids re-parsing the class on every recursive call. This is a small but real efficiency, and it keeps the recursion shallow in terms of work per frame.

The atom-kind parameter uses \texttt{'\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}'} as a sentinel for "this is a class". This is safe because a literal \texttt{'\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}'} cannot otherwise reach \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}: the parser in \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e} intercepts \texttt{'\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}'} and rewrites it into the class path before the atom is tested. Similarly, \texttt{'\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}'} is intercepted only in the wildcard sense; a literal dot would require an escape, which the engine does not provide.

\section{The \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e} Recursion}

The core of the engine is \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}. It takes two \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} arguments: the remaining pattern and the remaining input. It returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} if the remaining input is fully consumed by the remaining pattern. The function is recursive, with one recursive call per atom in the pattern, plus additional recursive calls in the quantifier branches. The verbatim definition is reproduced below; the remainder of this section walks through it clause by clause.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  static constexpr bool
  match_here (std::string_view pat, std::string_view str)
  {
    if (pat.empty ())
      return str.empty ();
    if (pat[0] == '^')
      return match_here (pat.substr (1), str);
    if (pat == "$")
      return str.empty ();

    // Parse class [...]
    std::string_view cls;
    std::size_t pat_advance = 1;
    char first = pat[0];
    if (first == '[')
      {
        auto end = pat.find (']', 1);
        if (end == std::string_view::npos)
          return false;
        cls = pat.substr (1, end - 1);
        pat_advance = end + 1;
      }

    // Check for quantifier
    char quant = 0;
    if (pat_advance < pat.size ()
        && (pat[pat_advance] == '*' || pat[pat_advance] == '+'))
      quant = pat[pat_advance++];

    auto rest = pat.substr (pat_advance);

    if (quant == '*')
      {
        // Try matching zero occurrences first, then one or more
        if (match_here (rest, str))
          return true;
        while (!str.empty () && match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
          {
            str.remove_prefix (1);
            if (match_here (rest, str))
              return true;
          }
        return false;
      }
    if (quant == '+')
      {
        // One or more
        if (str.empty () || !match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
          return false;
        str.remove_prefix (1);
        if (match_here (rest, str))
          return true;
        while (!str.empty () && match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
          {
            str.remove_prefix (1);
            if (match_here (rest, str))
              return true;
          }
        return false;
      }

    // Exactly one
    if (str.empty () || !match_one (first, cls, str[0]))
      return false;
    return match_here (rest, str.substr (1));
  }
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The function begins with the empty-pattern case, which requires empty input. A leading \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} is discarded because matching already starts at offset zero. A remaining pattern consisting only of \texttt{\$} also requires empty input, so a trailing end anchor never consumes a literal dollar sign.

After the base cases, the function parses the current atom. It records the first character of the pattern in \texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} and sets \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e} to one, the default advance for a single-character atom. If that first character is \texttt{[}, it searches for the matching \texttt{]} starting at position one. If no \texttt{]} is found the pattern is malformed and the function returns \texttt{f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}. Otherwise the class body is captured into \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s} and \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e} is advanced to the position just past the \texttt{]}. This parsing happens once per atom, not once per character, which is the efficiency motivation mentioned earlier.

With the atom parsed, the function checks for a trailing quantifier. It looks at the character immediately after the atom, which sits at index \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e}. If that character is \texttt{*} or \texttt{+}, it is recorded in \texttt{q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t} and \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}e} is advanced past it. The variable \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} is then bound to the pattern suffix that follows the atom (and its quantifier, if any). At this point the function has decomposed the pattern into three parts: the current atom (\texttt{f\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t}, optionally with \texttt{c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s}), an optional quantifier (\texttt{q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t}), and the rest of the pattern (\texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t}).

\section{The \texttt{*} Branch}

The \texttt{*} quantifier means "zero or more of the preceding atom". The implementation first tries the zero case: it recurses on \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} with the input unchanged. If that succeeds, the match is done and the function returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} immediately. This is the backtrack-friendly way of expressing that the atom is allowed to match nothing.

If the zero case fails, the function enters a \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e} loop that consumes input characters one at a time, provided each satisfies the atom via \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}. After each successful consumption it recurses on \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} with the shortened input. As soon as a recursion succeeds, the function returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}. If the loop exhausts either the input or the matching characters without a successful recursion, the function returns \texttt{f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}.

This loop tests possible repetition lengths in ascending order. If consuming three characters permits the suffix to match, it succeeds without requiring a four-character repetition. Since the API returns only a Boolean and has no captures, this search order does not change the recognized language.

The loop may retry a suffix many times. Multiple overlapping quantified atoms can multiply those retries; there is no general quadratic bound. Keep this matcher for small classification patterns and use the NFA-based \texttt{S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}R\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x} for larger inputs.

The order of the two strategies matters. Trying the zero case first means that an empty match is preferred whenever it allows the rest of the pattern to succeed. For a pattern such as \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}x} against the input \texttt{"\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}"}, the engine first tries matching \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}*} against the empty prefix, which leaves \texttt{x} to match the literal \texttt{x}, and succeeds immediately. Trying the greedy case first would have consumed the \texttt{x} and then failed to match the trailing literal, requiring a backtrack. The zero-first ordering avoids that backtrack in this common case.

\section{The \texttt{+} Branch}

The \texttt{+} quantifier means "one or more of the preceding atom". Its implementation is similar to the \texttt{*} branch but with the zero case removed. The function first requires that the input is non-empty and that its first character satisfies the atom; if not, the match fails immediately. This is the difference between \texttt{+} and \texttt{*}: \texttt{+} demands at least one match, \texttt{*} permits none.

After consuming the mandatory first character, the function tests the suffix immediately. If needed, it consumes more matching characters one at a time and retries the suffix after each. This gives \texttt{+} the same backtracking completeness as \texttt{*}, with a minimum repetition count of one.

For example, \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}9} matches \texttt{"\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}"}: the repetition consumes the first nine and the suffix consumes the second. \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}z} similarly matches \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}"}. Both C++ and D recognize these cases, and the regression suite checks them.

\section{The Exactly-One Fallthrough}

If no quantifier is present, the function falls through to the exactly-one case. This is the simplest path. It requires that the input is non-empty and that its first character satisfies the atom via \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}. If so, it recurses on \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t} with the input shortened by one character. If not, it returns \texttt{f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e}.

This is the path taken by literal characters, by \texttt{.} wildcards without a quantifier, and by character classes without a quantifier. The recursion advances the pattern by one atom and the input by one character, maintaining the invariant that the remaining input must be exactly what the remaining pattern describes. The base case at the top of the function — empty pattern implies empty input — terminates the recursion when all atoms have been consumed.

Because each recursive call in this branch advances the pattern by exactly one atom, the depth of recursion is bounded by the number of atoms in the pattern. For a pattern of length N the recursion depth is at most N. This makes the exactly-one path predictable and cheap, and it is the path that the overwhelming majority of atoms in a typical pattern take.

The trailing \texttt{\$} is handled by the earlier end-anchor base case. Thus \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+\allowbreak{}\$} and \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+} recognize the same whole-input strings.

\section{Repetition Search and Backtracking}

Both repetition operators test the suffix at every admissible repetition length. The C++ implementation searches shorter lengths first; D searches longer lengths first. With whole-input Boolean matching and no captures, these strategies produce the same result.

For most classification patterns, the rest of the pattern is empty or a fixed literal, and the distinction is invisible. The interesting cases arise when a quantifier is followed by another atom that overlaps the quantified class. Consider the pattern \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}*\allowbreak{}z} against the input \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}"}. The \texttt{*} branch first tries the zero case: matching \texttt{z} against \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}"} fails, because the first character is \texttt{a}. The loop then consumes \texttt{a}, retries \texttt{z} against \texttt{"\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}"} (fails), consumes \texttt{b}, retries \texttt{z} against \texttt{"\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}"} (succeeds), and returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}. The engine finds the split because of the per-consumption recursion in the \texttt{*} branch.

The same pattern against \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}"} fails because no split leaves exactly the suffix \texttt{"\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}"}. A candidate that leaves \texttt{"\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}"} also fails: whole-input matching requires consuming the final \texttt{c}.

The takeaway for users is that \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} implements a small, predictable subset of regex backtracking. It is sufficient for the lexical-classification tasks it was designed for, and it behaves consistently across all inputs. Where richer semantics are required — non-greedy quantifiers, alternation, grouping — the PEG engine in Chapters 27 and 28 is the appropriate tool.

\section{Integration with \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} and \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h}}

The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} template is designed to be used as the key of a \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} clause. Chapter 8 covered the consumer side in detail; this section covers the producer side, that is, what \texttt{W\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e} does when it encounters a pattern key. The relevant code is the \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} check at the top of \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
    using KeyT = std::remove_cvref_t<decltype (Key)>;
    if constexpr (requires { KeyT::matches (std::string_view{}); })
      {
        if constexpr (std::convertible_to<K, std::string_view>)
