The detection is structural rather than nominal. \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e} does not ask whether \texttt{K\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}T} is a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>} instantiation; it asks whether \texttt{K\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}T} exposes a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{})} member. Any type that satisfies this requirement is treated as a pattern key. This is a deliberate design choice: it allows users to plug in their own matcher types, provided they expose the same \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{})} shape. The \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} template is the canonical such type, but it is not the only one the dispatch machinery will accept.

When the \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} clause is satisfied, \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e} further requires that the runtime key be convertible to \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w}. This is what makes pattern-based dispatch usable with \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}*}, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g}, \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w}, and any other string-like type. The key is converted to a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} and passed to \texttt{K\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}T\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})}. If the match succeeds, the handler is invoked with the original key (not the converted view) and any trailing arguments, and the clause returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e}. If the match fails, the clause returns \texttt{f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} and the dispatch table proceeds to the next clause.

This routing is what allows a single \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table to mix value-based and pattern-based clauses freely. Value-based clauses use the \texttt{e\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f} branch with \texttt{e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h}; pattern-based clauses use the \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f} branch with \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}. The two are mutually exclusive at compile time: a key type either has a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} member or it does not. There is no ambiguity, and there is no run-time cost to the dispatch, because the \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} discards the unused branch at compile time.

\section{A First Example: Classifying Integers and Words}

The simplest use of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is direct invocation of \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} from a lambda or function. The example shipped as \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}4\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} shows the canonical pattern: a small lambda that tests an input against several patterns in sequence and returns a label.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto classify = [](std::string_view s) {
    if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(s)) return "int";
    if (dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">::matches(s)) return "word";
    return "other";
  };
  std::cout << classify("123") << " " << classify("abc") << "\n";
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The patterns here are mutually exclusive by construction. A string of digits satisfies \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+} and not \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+}. A string of lowercase letters satisfies \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+} and not \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+}. A string containing any other character, such as \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}"} or \texttt{"\allowbreak{}@\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}"}, satisfies neither and falls through to the \texttt{"\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}"} return. Because matching is anchored, \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}"} does not match \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+} even though it begins with letters; the entire input must be described by the pattern.

The order of the tests matters. A string such as \texttt{"\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}2\allowbreak{}3\allowbreak{}"} matches the first pattern and short-circuits; the second test is never run. Reordering the tests would change the result for inputs that could match more than one pattern, although in this particular example the patterns are disjoint. In general, when patterns overlap, the first matching clause wins, exactly as in a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} statement with a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}t}.

This style of classification is appropriate when the set of categories is small and fixed. For larger or more dynamic taxonomies, embedding the patterns in a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table is preferable, because it lets the dispatch machinery handle the ordering and fallthrough uniformly. That style is shown below in the discussion of example 19.

\section{Identifiers and Keywords}

A more realistic lexical classification distinguishes identifiers, keywords, and numbers. Identifiers in most C-like languages match the pattern \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}*}: an initial letter or underscore, followed by zero or more letters, underscores, or digits. Keywords are a fixed set of literal strings. Numbers match \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+}. The example shipped as \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} combines all three.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  auto classify_by_key = [](std::string_view token) {
    if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches (token))
      return LexemeClass::Number;
    if (dsl::pattern<"if">::matches (token)
        || dsl::pattern<"for">::matches (token)
        || dsl::pattern<"while">::matches (token))
      return LexemeClass::Keyword;
    if (dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches (token))
      return LexemeClass::Identifier;
    return LexemeClass::Other;
  };
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The number test comes first because it is the cheapest and the most specific. The keyword test comes second and consists of three literal patterns combined by \texttt{|\allowbreak{}|}. Each literal pattern is a \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>} instantiation with a fixed string; \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} returns \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} only when the input is exactly that string, because of the end-anchoring. The identifier test comes last and is the most general; it would also match the keywords, which is why the keyword test must precede it.

The identifier pattern is a good illustration of how atoms and quantifiers compose in this engine. The first atom is the class \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}]}, which matches one letter or underscore. The second atom is the class \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]} followed by the \texttt{*} quantifier, which matches zero or more word characters. The engine parses this as two atoms: a single class, and a class-with-quantifier. There is no grouping construct, but none is needed, because the quantifier already applies to the immediately preceding atom, which is exactly the second class.

The keyword test uses three separate \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiations rather than a single pattern with alternation. This is because the engine does not support the \texttt{|} operator. Users who need alternation within a single pattern must either enumerate the alternatives as separate \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiations combined by \texttt{|\allowbreak{}|}, as shown here, or move to the PEG engine. For small fixed keyword sets the enumeration approach is clear and efficient, and it has the added benefit of making each keyword a distinct type, which can be useful for diagnostics.

\section{Tokenizer-Kind Classification}

The example shipped as \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} shows the same classification idea in an even more compact form, returning string labels rather than enum values.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
#include "MuDSL.hpp"
#include <iostream>

int main() {
  auto token_kind = [](std::string_view t) {
    if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(t)) return "INT";
    if (dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">::matches(t)) return "ID";
    return "UNK";
  };
  std::cout << token_kind("abc") << " " << token_kind("99") << "\n";
}
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

This example is deliberately minimal. It shows that \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is usable as a one-liner predicate, with no setup, no state, and no dependencies beyond the header itself. The two patterns are disjoint and the fallthrough case returns a generic label. The example is a useful starting point for users who want to experiment with the matcher before integrating it into a larger dispatch table.

The simplicity of this example also highlights a limitation. Because the engine does not support \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}d} or other shorthand classes, every classification pattern must spell out its character ranges explicitly. A pattern such as \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+} will not match uppercase letters, digits, or underscores; if those should be classified as identifiers, the pattern must be widened, as in the \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}A\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}Z\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}*} form used in the previous example. Choosing the right pattern is a matter of understanding the lexical rules of the language being classified.

\section{Patterns Inside a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} Table}

The most powerful use of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is as a key inside a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table. In this style, the dispatch machinery itself runs the pattern tests, and the user supplies only the handlers. The example from \texttt{e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}/\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}1\allowbreak{}5\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}p} continues by defining a dispatch table over the \texttt{L\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s} enum, where the classification has already been performed by the lambda shown above. The table itself uses value-based clauses for the enum, but the same machinery supports pattern-based clauses directly.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  auto dispatch = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Number> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":number(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Keyword> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":keyword(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<LexemeClass::Identifier> (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":identifier(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (LexemeClass, std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":other(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

In this example the pattern-based classification and the value-based dispatch are kept separate, with the classification lambda producing an enum that the table then consumes. This separation is a matter of style. A more compact design would place the patterns directly in the table, as in the following sketch, which the \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}-based detection in \texttt{t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e} fully supports.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
  auto dispatch = dsl::match (
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">> (
          [] (std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":number(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::when<dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">> (
          [] (std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":identifier(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }),
      dsl::otherwise (
          [] (std::string_view token, std::string_view ctx)
          { return std::string (ctx) + ":other(" + std::string (token) + ")"; }));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

Here the table is invoked directly with the token string. The first clause whose pattern matches the token executes its handler; if none match, the \texttt{o\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e} clause runs. The order of the clauses is the order of the tests, so a more specific pattern must precede a more general one that would also match the same input. This is the same ordering discipline required by any pattern-matching dispatch, and it is the responsibility of the user, not the engine.

The \texttt{r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}q\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s}-based detection ensures that the \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}>} clauses are routed to the \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} call, while a \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}>} clause in the same table would be routed to the equality comparison. The two styles can be mixed in a single table, although doing so is unusual because the key types would have to agree across clauses. In practice, a table is either fully value-based or fully pattern-based; the engine supports both uniformly.

\section{Worked Examples of Pattern Matching}

This section presents a series of small examples that illustrate the matching semantics in isolation. Each example shows a pattern, an input, and the result, with a brief explanation of why the engine produces that result. These examples are intended to build a precise intuition for the engine's behaviour.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches("123"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(""));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches("12a"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[0-9]*">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-z]*">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-z]*">::matches("hello"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[a-z]*">::matches("Hello"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The first assertion holds because \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}+} requires at least one digit and the input is all digits. The second fails because \texttt{+} requires at least one. The third fails because end-anchoring demands that the entire input be digits, and the trailing \texttt{a} breaks the match. The fourth and fifth hold because \texttt{*} permits zero matches. The sixth holds because the input is all lowercase letters. The seventh fails because the initial uppercase \texttt{H} is not in the class \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]}.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches("alpha_7"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches("_"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"[a-zA-Z_][a-zA-Z_0-9]*">::matches("7alpha"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<".">::matches("x"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<".">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<".*">::matches(""));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<".*">::matches("anything"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The identifier pattern matches \texttt{"\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}7\allowbreak{}"} because the first character is a letter and the rest are word characters. It matches \texttt{"\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}"} because the first character is an underscore and the \texttt{*} permits the rest to be empty. It fails to match \texttt{"\allowbreak{}7\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}"} because the first character is a digit, which is not in the initial class. The wildcard examples show that \texttt{.} requires exactly one character and \texttt{.\allowbreak{}*} accepts any string, including the empty string.

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"if">::matches("if"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"if">::matches("iffy"));
static_assert(!dsl::pattern<"if">::matches("If"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"^if">::matches("if"));
static_assert( dsl::pattern<"if$">::matches("if"));
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The literal-pattern examples show whole-input matching: \texttt{"\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}"} matches \texttt{"\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}"}, while \texttt{"\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}"} and \texttt{"\allowbreak{}I\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}"} do not. The explicit-anchor forms \texttt{\textasciicircum{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f}, \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\$}, and \texttt{\textasciicircum{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\$} also match \texttt{"\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}"}. A trailing \texttt{\$} requires the input to have ended; it does not require a dollar-sign byte.

\section{Limitations of the Engine}

The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} engine is intentionally limited. This section catalogues the limitations honestly, so that users can decide whether the engine is appropriate for a given task. The limitations are not defects; they are design choices that keep the engine small and predictable. Users who need richer semantics should use the PEG engine described in Chapters 27 and 28.

The engine does not support alternation. There is no \texttt{|} operator, and therefore no way to write a single pattern that matches either \texttt{"\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}"} or \texttt{"\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}"}. The workaround is to use multiple \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiations combined by \texttt{|\allowbreak{}|} at the C++ level, as shown in the keyword example above. This is slightly more verbose but has the advantage of making each alternative a distinct type, which can aid diagnostics.

The engine does not support grouping. There are no parentheses, and therefore no way to apply a quantifier to a sub-expression of more than one atom. The pattern \texttt{(\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}*} is not expressible. This is a more fundamental restriction than the lack of alternation, because there is no simple workaround at the C++ level. Users who need to recognise repeated multi-character sequences must use the PEG engine.

The engine does not support backreferences. There is no capture, and therefore no way to refer to a previously matched substring later in the pattern. This is consistent with the engine's pure-function design: a match is a function of pattern and input, with no state carried between calls. Backreferences are incompatible with that design.

The engine does not support shorthand classes. There is no \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}d}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}w}, \texttt{\textbackslash{}\allowbreak{}s}, or any other backslash escape. Every class must spell out its characters or ranges explicitly. There is also no escape mechanism for matching special characters literally; the characters \texttt{.}, \texttt{[}, \texttt{]}, \texttt{*}, \texttt{+}, \texttt{\textasciicircum{}}, \texttt{\$}, and \texttt{-} cannot be matched as literals in positions where the parser would interpret them as operators. Inside a class, \texttt{-} is the only character with a special meaning, and only when it appears between two other characters.

The engine does not support counted repetition. There is no \texttt{\{\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}\}}, \texttt{\{\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}\}}, or \texttt{\{\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{},\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}\}} form. A pattern that requires exactly three digits must be written as \texttt{[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}0\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}9\allowbreak{}]}. This is verbose but straightforward for small counts, and it is adequate for the lexical-classification tasks the engine is designed for.

The engine does not support the \texttt{?} quantifier. There is no zero-or-one operator. A pattern that permits an optional character must be expressed by other means, typically by listing the variants explicitly or by restructuring the surrounding dispatch. The \texttt{*} quantifier can sometimes substitute, but it permits more than one occurrence, which is not always acceptable.

Classes cannot nest. The engine finds the first \texttt{]} after the opening \texttt{[} and treats everything in between as the class body. There is no support for \texttt{[\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}\&\allowbreak{}\&\allowbreak{}[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}]\allowbreak{}]} or any other nested form. The class body is a flat sequence of singletons and ranges. This is sufficient for the character sets that arise in lexical classification, but it precludes more sophisticated class expressions.

Contrasting with full POSIX or ECMAScript regex, the \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} engine omits: alternation, grouping, backreferences, shorthand classes, counted repetition, non-greedy quantifiers, case-insensitive matching, multiline mode, lookahead, lookbehind, and Unicode character properties. What it provides is a small, fast, compile-time-evaluable matcher for anchored full matches over a tiny but useful regex subset. For tasks within its scope it is adequate and convenient; for tasks beyond its scope the PEG engine is the recommended alternative.

\section{Choosing Between \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} and the PEG Engine}

The library offers two recognition engines: the \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} engine described in this chapter, and the PEG engine described in Chapters 27 and 28. The two are complementary, and the choice between them depends on the task. \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is appropriate when the recognition task is a single anchored classification predicate, when the pattern is short and fixed, and when the set of categories is small. The PEG engine is appropriate when the recognition task involves sequencing, alternation, or recursion, when the grammar is too complex to express as a single regex, or when the result of recognition is a structured parse tree rather than a boolean.

A useful rule of thumb is to reach for \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} first, and to move to the PEG engine only when \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} proves insufficient. The \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} engine is smaller, simpler, and has no dependencies beyond the header itself. Its matching algorithm is easy to reason about, and its limitations are easy to work around for the kinds of tasks it was designed for. The PEG engine is more powerful but also more complex, and it brings its own concepts of grammar definition, combinator composition, and parse-tree construction.

In a typical MuDSL workflow, \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is used in the early stages of a pipeline, where tokens are classified into kinds, and the PEG engine is used in later stages, where the token stream is parsed into a tree. The two engines share no code but coexist peacefully in the same header. A program can use both without conflict, and the choice of engine for a given task is purely a matter of expressiveness and convenience.

\section{A Note on \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} Evaluability}

Every member of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}, and the helpers it calls are likewise \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}. This means that a match can be performed entirely at compile time, provided the input is a compile-time constant. The \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}t} examples shown earlier in this chapter are not merely illustrative; they are genuine compile-time tests that the compiler evaluates during template instantiation.

Compile-time matching is useful for asserting invariants about literal strings embedded in a program. For example, a library author might assert that a particular configuration string matches an expected pattern, with the check performed at build time and with no run-time cost. The same machinery can be used in \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l} functions to construct compile-time lookup tables indexed by pattern.

The cost of compile-time matching is borne by the compiler, and for very long inputs or pathological patterns the compile time can become noticeable. For typical lexical-classification patterns and short inputs the cost is negligible. Users who observe slow compiles should check whether a \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiation is being invoked repeatedly in a tight \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} context, and consider hoisting the result into a named \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r} variable.

At run time, the matcher is a plain function with no virtual dispatch, no allocation, and no exceptions. It can be called from hot loops without concern. The recursion depth is bounded by the pattern length, and the work per recursive call is a small constant. For the inputs the engine is designed for, performance is not a consideration.

\section{Composing Patterns with Operators}

Although \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} itself has no composition operators, the surrounding \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l} namespace provides facilities for combining predicates. Chapter 7 covers the operators feature in detail, including the predicate-composition operators that can be used to build compound matchers from \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiations. The typical pattern is to wrap a \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s} call in a predicate and then combine predicates with \texttt{\&\allowbreak{}\&} or \texttt{|\allowbreak{}|}.

This style of composition is more verbose than writing a single regex with alternation, but it has the advantage of preserving the type identity of each component pattern. Each \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiation remains a distinct type, which can be useful for diagnostics and for dispatch. The composition happens at the predicate level, not at the pattern level, and the resulting predicate can be used anywhere a boolean predicate is accepted.

For the simple classification tasks that are the primary use case of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>}, composition is rarely needed. The patterns are usually disjoint, and a sequence of \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f} tests is the clearest expression of the classification. Composition becomes more valuable when patterns overlap in complex ways, or when the same pattern is reused across multiple classifiers. In those cases the predicate-composition operators provide a clean way to build reusable building blocks.

\section{Diagnostics and the \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} Member}

The \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} member of \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} is provided primarily for diagnostics. Because it is a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w}, it can be included in error messages, log lines, and debug output without any run-time cost. A typical use is in a fallback handler that reports which patterns were tried and failed, to assist the user in understanding why a given input was classified as "other".

\begingroup
\newminted[MDCode]{cpp}{}
\begin{MDCode}
auto report = [](std::string_view input) {
  if (dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::matches(input)) return "number";
  if (dsl::pattern<"[a-z]+">::matches(input)) return "word";
  return "other";
};
// In a diagnostics path:
std::cerr << "pattern value: " << dsl::pattern<"[0-9]+">::value << "\n";
\end{MDCode}
\endgroup

The \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} member is also useful when constructing dispatch tables programmatically. A table-builder that accepts \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} instantiations can expose the pattern text to the user for confirmation, without having to parse the template arguments. This is a small convenience, but it makes the engine slightly easier to use in metaprogramming contexts where the pattern text is not otherwise visible.

Because \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} is a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} into the \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} NTTP, it is valid for the lifetime of the program. There is no dangling-pointer risk, no matter how long the \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} is held. This is a consequence of the NTTP storage model: the pattern text lives in static storage for the entire program, and the \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} merely points into it.

\section{Summary}

\texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}>} is a small, compile-time-evaluable regex-subset matcher parameterised on a \texttt{F\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}S\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g} NTTP. It exposes a \texttt{v\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}e} member for diagnostics and a \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{})} member for testing whether an input satisfies the pattern. Matching is anchored at both ends: the entire input must be described by the pattern. The supported syntax comprises the wildcard \texttt{.}, character classes \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}]}, ranges \texttt{[\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}-\allowbreak{}z\allowbreak{}]} within classes, the \texttt{*} and \texttt{+} postfix quantifiers, and the \texttt{\textasciicircum{}} and \texttt{\$} anchors (which are accepted but not meaningful). The engine is implemented by three private helpers: \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}s}, which scans a class body for singletons and ranges; \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}e}, which dispatches on the atom kind; and \texttt{m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}e}, the recursive matcher that parses atoms, applies quantifiers with greedy backtracking semantics, and enforces end-anchoring.

Integration with the dispatch machinery of Chapter 8 is structural: any type exposing a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}b\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w\allowbreak{})} member is treated as a pattern key by \texttt{W\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}C\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}u\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}y\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}k\allowbreak{}e}, which converts the runtime key to a \texttt{s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}g\allowbreak{}\_\allowbreak{}\allowbreak{}v\allowbreak{}i\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}w} and delegates to the matcher. This lets \texttt{w\allowbreak{}h\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}.\allowbreak{}"\allowbreak{}>\allowbreak{}>} clauses sit alongside value-based clauses in a \texttt{d\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}l\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}m\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}h} table, with the routing decided at compile time by \texttt{i\allowbreak{}f\allowbreak{}\ \allowbreak{}c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}.

The engine is deliberately limited. It omits alternation, grouping, backreferences, shorthand classes, counted repetition, the \texttt{?} quantifier, nested classes, and case-insensitive matching. These omissions keep the engine small and predictable, and they scope it to the lexical-classification tasks for which it was designed. For richer recognition, including sequencing, alternation, and recursive grammars, the PEG engine of Chapters 27 and 28 is the appropriate tool. Within its scope, \texttt{p\allowbreak{}a\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}r\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}<\allowbreak{}>} provides a fast, allocation-free, \texttt{c\allowbreak{}o\allowbreak{}n\allowbreak{}s\allowbreak{}t\allowbreak{}e\allowbreak{}x\allowbreak{}p\allowbreak{}r}-evaluable matcher that is convenient to use and easy to reason about.
